WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.050 --> 00:00:05.230
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here
and in this video I'm revealing how the ending

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:08.920
to Shane Black's The Predator was completely
changed plus all the other deleted scenes

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:10.020
you never got to see.

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.910
Of course, I'll be talking spoilers, so take
care if you haven't seen the movie yet.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:18.460
Let's kick things off with the scrapped ending
that would have featured Arnold Schwarznegger's

00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:22.810
Dutch making a surprise comeback to set up
The Predator's sequel in a completely different

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:25.019
way to what we saw in the final film.

00:00:25.019 --> 00:00:30.369
In Black's original 2016 screenplay, after
McKenna, Rory and Casey's final fight with

00:00:30.369 --> 00:00:32.710
the Ultimate Predator, a chopper turns up.

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:37.010
As it lands, we discover it's none other than
Schwarzenegger's Dutch who's described in

00:00:37.010 --> 00:00:40.300
the script as having a face that's "haunted
[and] etched by pain."

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:44.940
Dutch asks them to come with him and McKenna's
son Rory asks if he should go too.

00:00:44.940 --> 00:00:49.309
Dutch smiles and replies "especially you",
then the screen cuts to black.

00:00:49.309 --> 00:00:53.469
In this alternate ending, Dutch would also
have revealed that a war has started.

00:00:53.469 --> 00:00:57.340
It's not stated explicitly but it's heavily
implied that there's some kind of secret war

00:00:57.340 --> 00:00:59.909
going on between humanity and the Predators.

00:00:59.909 --> 00:01:04.379
I think we can see a remnant of this idea
in this next scene which shows McKenna looking

00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:06.660
up and saying "come get us mother*******".

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:10.450
However, although that scene was shot it was
deleted from the final film.

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:14.830
Now, I suspect many fans of the original movie
would have loved a Schwarzenegger cameo, but

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:18.770
he turned down the role because he said that
after reading the script he didn't like it

00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:21.150
and the part was too small.

00:01:21.150 --> 00:01:25.230
Schwarzenegger also seemed more interested
in being available for James Cameron's Terminator

00:01:25.230 --> 00:01:27.410
6 where he does have a significant role.

00:01:27.410 --> 00:01:31.820
Shane Black has said he understands why Schwarzenegger
rejected the role, but added that the studio

00:01:31.820 --> 00:01:36.090
didn't want the new movie to be centred around
the actor's return to the franchise and instead

00:01:36.090 --> 00:01:38.330
wanted to introduce a new cast.

00:01:38.330 --> 00:01:42.110
The script's alternate ending would have been
stronger in my opinion than the Predator Killer

00:01:42.110 --> 00:01:46.530
suit reveal which felt tacked on and tonally
didn't fit the rest of the movie.

00:01:46.530 --> 00:01:50.950
In fact, that moment in the final film felt
more like a Marvel post-credits scene for

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:54.970
an Iron Man movie because the way the tech
wrapped itself around the scientist's body

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.890
screamed Tony Stark's nanotech suit to me.

00:01:57.890 --> 00:02:01.730
Also, I have to wonder why the studio didn't
think of simply replacing Schwarzenegger's

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:05.200
cameo with say Danny Glover from Predator
2.

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:09.801
Black's original script also reveals another
deleted section from the last act of the film

00:02:09.801 --> 00:02:11.150
that we never got to see.

00:02:11.150 --> 00:02:15.620
In the original story, there was a group of
friendly Predators hidden away in Area 52

00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.780
who'd been working with the secret government
organisation to help defend the Earth against

00:02:19.780 --> 00:02:22.080
the incoming wave of Upgrade Predators.

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:26.380
In fact, they were actually going to fight
alongside McKenna's team and you can see these

00:02:26.380 --> 00:02:31.330
friendly Predators decked out in military
camouflage uniform in on-set photos.

00:02:31.330 --> 00:02:35.170
Trailers also show footage from those tank
scenes which never made it into the final

00:02:35.170 --> 00:02:36.210
movie.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:40.290
And in the original script, the Upgrade Predator
had also been tasked with tracking down and

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:42.800
killing those friendly Predators.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:47.190
Tied in with the original story about Predators
working with the government was Edward James

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:50.630
Olmos's character who ended up getting chopped
out of the movie.

00:02:50.630 --> 00:02:55.540
Olmos played a four-star general above Sterling
K Brown's character and it seems like he was

00:02:55.540 --> 00:02:57.920
going to be the real big villain of the movie.

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.761
According to Shane Black, Olmos's character
"was trying to recruit the first Predator

00:03:01.761 --> 00:03:04.540
that arrived on Earth, and it communicated
with him."

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:09.190
The actor was due to appear in two scenes,
but they were both deleted when Black decided

00:03:09.190 --> 00:03:14.010
he had one bad guy too many in the story and
so Sterling K Brown took over as the chief

00:03:14.010 --> 00:03:15.040
human antagonist.

00:03:15.040 --> 00:03:19.940
By the way, one of the movies Olmos is well
known for is Blade Runner where his character

00:03:19.940 --> 00:03:22.390
is known for making little origami unicorns.

00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:27.070
So, I wonder if Black included that little
tinfoil unicorn in The Predator as a kind

00:03:27.070 --> 00:03:31.570
of easter egg to Blade Runner as Olmos was
originally supposed to be in this movie.

00:03:31.570 --> 00:03:35.951
The Upgrade Predator was also going to unleash
even more Predator hybrids than the Predator

00:03:35.951 --> 00:03:37.319
dogs we saw in the film.

00:03:37.319 --> 00:03:41.220
We could have ended up seeing Predators with
multiple arms which to date have only appeared

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:42.430
in comics.

00:03:42.430 --> 00:03:46.000
And there were even going to be hybrid Predator
spiders!

00:03:46.000 --> 00:03:50.520
The Predator Killer ending in the final movie
is also part of a huge story change that was

00:03:50.520 --> 00:03:54.890
made when Shane Black decided to go back and
almost completely reshoot the entire third

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:55.890
act.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:59.651
The Predator Killer suit is revealed to be
a piece of technology that the first predator

00:03:59.651 --> 00:04:03.690
we see in the movie brought to Earth as a
gift to help humanity defend itself against

00:04:03.690 --> 00:04:08.020
the Upgrade Predators who want to move in
and colonise the planet because it's getting

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.020
warmer.

00:04:09.020 --> 00:04:12.650
The problem with this new ending is that it
doesn't match up very well with what happened

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:13.900
earlier in the film.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:18.019
If the fugitive predator came to Earth to
help humans out, then why was it slaughtering

00:04:18.019 --> 00:04:22.289
anyone it could lay its hands on right from
the moment it landed on Earth?

00:04:22.289 --> 00:04:26.340
Another reason why Shane Black says he reshot
almost the entire third act is that while

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:30.270
reviewing footage he thought the fight scenes
with the Predator in daylight didn't look

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:31.370
very good.

00:04:31.370 --> 00:04:34.949
Black explained that when they "started to
do [their] effects work, […] it looked kind

00:04:34.949 --> 00:04:35.949
of cheap in a way.

00:04:35.949 --> 00:04:39.430
It didn't really feel like a big expensive
movie when the predators [were] just running

00:04:39.430 --> 00:04:41.180
around in daylight like that."

00:04:41.180 --> 00:04:46.340
So, Black managed to convince Fox to reshoot
much of the final act in the dark, which must

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:50.889
have been pretty expensive, and is likely
why the film's release was put back from February

00:04:50.889 --> 00:04:53.479
to September 2018.

00:04:53.479 --> 00:04:57.689
Although I can see Black's point, I have to
wonder whether this was really a good idea

00:04:57.689 --> 00:05:01.580
because many people have complained about
it being really hard to figure out what was

00:05:01.580 --> 00:05:03.759
going on in the fight scenes at the end.

00:05:03.759 --> 00:05:08.449
The geography of the action as it cut between
characters was very confusing and the darkness

00:05:08.449 --> 00:05:10.939
of the footage definitely didn't help that.

00:05:10.939 --> 00:05:16.219
Funnily enough this isn't the first time similar
complaints have surfaced in the Predator franchise.

00:05:16.219 --> 00:05:20.900
One of the many criticisms levelled at Alien
vs Predator Requiem is that much of the action

00:05:20.900 --> 00:05:25.319
happens in the dark after a power outage,
making it incredibly difficult to distinguish

00:05:25.319 --> 00:05:28.370
what's going on in the action scenes.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:33.300
One very widely publicised deleted scene happened
when Olivia Munn discovered one her co-actors,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.310
Steven Wilder Striegel, was a convicted sex
offender.

00:05:36.310 --> 00:05:40.039
The scene in question cast Striegel in the
part of a stranger who tries to chat up Munn's

00:05:40.039 --> 00:05:41.699
character Casey in a dog park.

00:05:41.699 --> 00:05:45.340
It was certainly a sensible move to delete
the scene in question.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:49.129
What was lost with the cut was a longer scene
introducing Munn's character as someone who

00:05:49.129 --> 00:05:53.559
doesn't take any nonsense from anyone and
it also would have foreshadowed a little more

00:05:53.559 --> 00:05:55.849
her later connection with one of the Predator
dogs.

00:05:55.849 --> 00:06:00.520
So, I'm guessing the scene may have been reshot
if the issue had come to light earlier.

00:06:00.520 --> 00:06:04.051
Whether these alternate endings, stories and
deleted scenes would have made the movie any

00:06:04.051 --> 00:06:05.770
better is debatable.

00:06:05.770 --> 00:06:09.470
Although I've enjoyed some of Black's movies
in the past, I think his approach to this

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.389
Predator movie was flawed.

00:06:11.389 --> 00:06:15.270
It's on record that when Black came on board
the project as writer and director, one of

00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:19.719
his conditions was that everything had to
be bigger; a bigger budget, a bigger scope,

00:06:19.719 --> 00:06:22.300
more characters, more Predators, more of everything.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.370
And I think that led to too much being crammed
into the story.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:31.089
On top of that, because there were huge story
changes and extensive reshoots, some crucial

00:06:31.089 --> 00:06:36.320
aspects just don't make real sense any more,
such as the motives and actions of the first

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.699
Predator we see in the film.

00:06:37.699 --> 00:06:41.659
And then maybe because the whole thing is
such a hodge-podge of original and reshot

00:06:41.659 --> 00:06:45.939
footage, combined with editing that feels
too fast and frenetic, it means that moments

00:06:45.939 --> 00:06:50.270
like the death of Sterling K. Brown's character
happened so quickly that if you blinked you

00:06:50.270 --> 00:06:51.559
probably missed it.

00:06:51.559 --> 00:06:54.520
So, would you have preferred the original
ending to The Predator?

00:06:54.520 --> 00:06:58.409
And would you like to have seen any of these
deleted scenes in the final film?

00:06:58.409 --> 00:07:02.410
Leave your thoughts in the comments below
and let me know whether you enjoyed the movie.

